WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:05.149
There's a lot going on in Star Wars Episode
IX, which means that some characters were

00:00:05.149 --> 00:00:10.140
bound to fall by the wayside as others took
the lead in the closing chapter of the saga.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:14.480
Here are several characters with relatively
small roles in The Rise of Skywalker, along

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:17.700
with why each one ended up with such limited
screen time.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:24.239
The Force Awakens introduced viewers to an
older, wiser Leia Organa, now a general leading

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.220
the Resistance against the First Order.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:30.919
Leia continued in that role in The Last Jedi,
but actress Carrie Fisher sadly and suddenly

00:00:30.919 --> 00:00:34.530
died in 2016 after completing work on that
film.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:39.200
Director J.J. Abrams opted to use several
minutes of Leia footage left over from The

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:44.031
Force Awakens for The Rise of Skywalker, so
Leia is present throughout most of the film

00:00:44.031 --> 00:00:46.100
as the leader of the Resistance.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:50.360
But the limited Fisher footage meant that
she was often shown simply observing and reacting

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:51.760
to other characters.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.560
The dialogue scenes she does have are short,
and with the help of movie magic, Abrams engineered

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:59.200
a somber, meaningful death scene for her character.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:05.260
Lots of fan favorite characters from the original
trilogy, including Han Solo, Leia Organa,

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:08.320
and Luke Skywalker, returned in The Force
Awakens.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:12.840
But what about Han's old pal and the suavest
dude in the galaxy, Lando Calrissian?

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:17.760
Lando didn't make his return until The Rise
of Skywalker, and it's easy to see why.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:22.770
With both Han Solo and Luke Skywalker dead,
and Leia Organa pulling limited duty, the

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:25.400
film needed another Rebel legend to turn to.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:28.680
In the end, though, that's pretty much all
Lando is there for.

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:33.390
An intended subplot about Lando meeting his
long-lost daughter was reportedly cut out

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:38.370
of the film, leaving him to just look cool,
saying a few catchy lines, and be an inspirational

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.120
figure for the younger heroes.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:45.159
When it was announced that Mark Hamill would
return to reprise the role of Luke Skywalker

00:01:45.159 --> 00:01:49.940
for The Force Awakens, fans were excited,
but then learned he only appeared onscreen

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:51.280
in the final scene.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:56.450
The Last Jedi gave Luke a lot to do, but it
presented a divisive version of Luke Skywalker,

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:59.400
capping things off with the character's redemptive
death.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:03.900
Fans were excited to find out Hamill would
be back for The Rise of Skywalker, but surely

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:07.340
he couldn't do that much as a Force ghost,
right?

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:12.170
He does appear a key moment in the story,
when Rey is doubting herself and contemplating

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:14.800
a self-exile similar to Luke's own.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:18.240
He dispenses some wisdom then shuffles back
off into the afterlife.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:22.260
After that, he does some voiceover, and that's
the entirety of his role.

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:27.800
In The Last Jedi, one of the most important
new characters was Rose Tico, a Resistance

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:31.550
mechanic who ran into Finn and struck up a
friendship that led to a mission and even

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:33.220
a hint of romance.

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:37.080
Rose shows back up in Rise as a mainstay of
the Resistance.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:41.320
Unfortunately, that role apparently also came
at the cost of her screen time.

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:45.810
As Rey, Finn, and Poe head out for their mission
early in the film, Rose explains that she

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:48.860
can't come because Leia has asked her to stay
behind.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:52.700
She then disappears for much of the rest of
the film before fighting in the Battle of

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:53.770
Exegol.

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:58.480
According to co-writer Chris Terrio, Rose's
role in the film was initially beefier, but

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:01.020
had to be cut down because of special effects
issues.

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.020
He told AwardsDaily,

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:07.900
“We wanted Rose to be the anchor at the
rebel base who was with Leia...As the process

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:12.760
evolved, a few scenes we'd written with Rose
and Leia turned out to not meet the standard

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:15.170
of photorealism that we'd hoped for.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:18.040
Those scenes unfortunately fell out of the
film.”

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:23.040
Maz Kanata's presence in The Force Awakens
raised a number of questions about why the

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:28.379
cantina owner and former smuggler knew so
much about the Force and how she had possession

00:03:28.379 --> 00:03:30.239
of Luke Skywalker's lightsaber.

00:03:30.239 --> 00:03:32.670
Those questions never really got answered.

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:35.040
After a brief appearance in The Last Jedi...

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.300
"Maz, what is happening?"

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:38.000
"Union dispute.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.800
You do not want to hear about it."

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:46.360
Maz appears at Resistance base in The Rise
of Skywalker, where she stays for pretty much

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:49.950
the entire movie without really contributing
much to the plot.

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:54.629
She functions almost as a Greek chorus, using
her wisdom and knowledge of the Force to help

00:03:54.629 --> 00:03:58.590
give the audience some context for Leia's
final struggle, then popping up during the

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:01.200
final celebration to give Chewbacca a medal.

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:06.480
When Finn, Poe, and Rey make it to the Endor
system, they come across a group of warriors

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.440
who agree to help them get to the debris of
the second Death Star to find what they're

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:11.550
looking for.

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:16.900
These warriors are later revealed to be deserter
stormtroopers who've made a life for themselves,

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:20.340
stripping their old First Order ship for parts
to get by.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:25.460
Their leader, Jannah, has a conversation with
Finn about the shared aspects of their pasts.

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:29.540
Jannah's a fascinating character with a lot
of potential depth, but she doesn't get a

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:31.140
really big chunk of screentime.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:35.699
She gets early moments on Endor establishing
who she is, then hangs around with Finn throughout

00:04:35.699 --> 00:04:39.870
the final battle, but we don't really learn
what her real desires are other than to fight

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:40.889
the First Order.

00:04:40.889 --> 00:04:45.420
There's a hint of what she does next in a
very ambiguous scene with Lando at the end

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:50.000
of the film, a likely holdover from the cut
subplot where she was meant to be Lando's

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:50.700
daughter.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:55.560
When Finn, Poe, and Rey head out for their
mission to find Palpatine, the gang takes

00:04:55.560 --> 00:05:01.700
C-3PO and BB-8 along for the trip, but leave
R2-D2 behind at the Resistance base.

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:06.880
Artoo is thus relatively quiet until he flies
into battle with Poe at the end of the film.

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:11.960
Why leave Artoo, arguably the most iconic
and beloved droid in the franchise, back at

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:13.500
the base for much of the film?

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:18.060
Well, in many ways Artoo has always felt like
Luke Skywalker's droid, and with Luke out

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:22.220
of the picture for so much of the sequel trilogy,
Artoo doesn't always fit.

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:27.020
As it turns out, Rise of Skywalker is much
more of a showcase for Threepio's place in

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:28.380
Star Wars lore.

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:32.940
And considering BB-8 has become the unofficial
mascot of the sequel trilogy, the filmmakers

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:37.750
likely chose to give a little more screentime
to that little toyetic ball droid to close

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:38.750
out the series.

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:40.160
"Where do you come from?"

